    "body": "Thank you for reporting!\r\n\r\n> For an inline script element, it looks like nonce & hash checks occur during “prepare a script” based on the text content of the element at that time. However even with synchronous scripts, the text content may be mutated prior to evaluation\r\n\r\nYes.\r\n\r\n> in the spec that would invalidate a prior successful check if this occurs.\r\n\r\nThe text content at the time of `#prepare-a-script` (== `source text` in the `#prepare-a-script` spec) is the source of truth, both for CSP hash check and evaluation, and the updated text content is not used for evaluation nor CSP hash check.\r\n`source text` is plumbed via `#creating-a-classic-script` in Step 27.2.\r\n\r\nI'm not sure when the `MutationObserver` callbacks should be executed, but based on the behavior reported, I suspect the callbacks are executed just before `#prepare-a-script` and thus the updated text content is used for evaluation for the nonce case and is rejected by the CSP check for the hash case.\r\n\r\nI think the behavior on Firefox that allows the updated \"hashed\" text content evaluation is a bug.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
