    "body": "> I suspect the callbacks are executed just before #prepare-a-script and thus the updated text content is used for evaluation for the nonce case and is rejected by the CSP check for the hash case.\r\n\r\nThat would explain the behavior in Chromium, yep — ~but given prepare-a-script happens [immediately](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#immediately) upon insertion and the mutation record reactions are only [queued](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#queue-a-mutation-observer-compound-microtask) as microtasks, the mutations in the example would have to occur after prepare-a-script. This suggests Firefox’s behavior is what’s aligned with what’s currently specified.~ This was [incorrect](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/7882#issuecomment-1115555496).\r\n\r\n(Custom element reactions and legacy DOM mutation events also provide notable hooks here and `<script is=\"custom-elem\">` might present a particularly interesting interleaving scenario. Also FWIW: trusted-types _does_ seem to catch this as a sink — impressive!)",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
