    "created_at": "2022-05-02T23:32:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-05-02T23:39:05Z",
    "body": "> Chromium works as specified and Firefox and Safari don't.\r\n\r\nI believe none of them work as specified(?) I now see that prepare-a-script should be closing over the original source text via [create a classic script](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#creating-a-classic-script). All three browsers are failing to do this in _both_ cases:\r\n\r\n- the nonce and hash scripts _should_ both pass their CSP checks during preparation, but the Script Record should preserve the original source text\r\n- subsequent modifications are, well, subsequent! the script record produced in the preparation step should not be impacted by them\r\n- both scripts (i.e., the classic script records — that’s what’s being queued) should run, but _without_ the injected code.\r\n\r\nIf the check fails (chrome+hash), preparation happened at the wrong time. if the check passes but the injected text evaluates (chrome+nonce, FF & Safari in all cases), preparation happened at the right time, but evaluation happened with the wrong text. Am I missing something that makes Chrome’s behavior correct in either the hash or nonce cases?",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
